<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Lonnie found the first one behind the bar, five minutes before opening, when the lights were still set to Forgiving and the floor still smelled like last night\'92s spilled Choke cola and someone else\'92s dignity.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He was lying flat on his back in the staff corridor with a headset on, frothing at the mouth like a man auditioning for a medical pamphlet. The headphones were plugged into one of those prehistoric music devices tourists brought down to the Ghetto for the thrill of holding something with buttons.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie stared for half a second too long. She had seen a lot in the Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill\'97she had once watched an Elvis attempt to swallow a billiard ball to prove masculinity was a spectrum\'97and this still managed to be new.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oi,\'94 she said, because in a crisis you started with the smallest word and worked up. \'93You right there, sugar?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvis on the floor jerked and made a sound like a bad amplifier. His fingers clawed the air. His eyes were open, unfocused, looking at something on the ceiling that wasn\'92t there.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>From the speakers came a thin, tinny harmony\'97gospel, sweet as syrup, crawling under the skin.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie\'92s mouth went dry.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Two more staff drifted in, drawn by the noise like flies to a regrettable fruit bowl. One of them\'97big, shiny, hair perfect\'97knelt and tried to pull the headphones off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The moment the pads lifted, the kneeling Elvis\'92 face changed. His eyes widened and he vomited with abrupt sincerity, then scrambled backwards on his palms as if the song itself had teeth.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Taxing gospel,\'94 he rasped, wiping his mouth with the back of his wrist. He looked at Lonnie as if she\'92d served it to him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie didn\'92t take her eyes off the device. \'93Who put that on?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I thought it was a warm-up track,\'94 another Elvis said, panicked and defensive. \'93It was in the jukebox crate.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie froze.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The jukebox crate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta\'92s regular booth was still set up in the centre of the room\'97of course it was; the booth was basically structural at this point\'97and the stage lights were already breathing softly, ready to make someone feel special. Over by the wall, the Seeburg Select-O-Matic waited with its glass face and its carousel of 45s, looking innocent in the way machinery did right before it ruined your life.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie strode out into the bar proper and yanked the curtain cord so the \'93Forgiving\'94 lights snapped into something less flattering. She crossed to the jukebox and opened its front door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, the records were wrong.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Not wrong as in scratched or dirty\'97that was normal. Wrong as in unfamiliar labels. Wrong as in the kind of tidy, earnest typography you saw on church pamphlets. Wrong as in a title that had no business in the Memphis Mafia unless it was being mocked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>AMAZING GRACE.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie felt her stomach drop and then, immediately after, harden into anger.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You,\'94 she called over her shoulder, pointing without looking. \'93Get the power cord.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A junior Elvis\'97still pale from vomiting\'97grabbed the cord like it was a snake and hesitated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Unplug it,\'94 Lonnie snapped. \'93Unless you want me to unplug your spine.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He yanked. The jukebox died with a soft mechanical sigh. The bar fell into a quiet so sudden it felt like pressure.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind her, in the corridor, the Elvis on the floor stopped jerking.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>That should have been relief.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Instead, it was worse.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Because he didn\'92t get up. He didn\'92t laugh it off. He didn\'92t swear, or demand a beer, or try to throw a pool cue at someone to reassert his dignity. He lay there, breathing hard, and then began to whisper.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie walked back and crouched beside him.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>His lips were moving fast now, words tumbling out like he\'92d been holding them in for years and someone had finally taken the lid off.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Praise be,\'94 he murmured, hoarse. \'93Praise be to\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie flinched. She didn\'92t like hearing those words in that place. Those words belonged upstairs. In Airborne offices. In sermons about profit. Not here.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Another Elvis backed away, eyes darting. \'93He\'92s\'85 he\'92s doing it. He\'92s doing the thing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The thing?\'94 Lonnie demanded.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Evangelism,\'94 someone said, as if naming it made it real and therefore, somehow, manageable.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The whispering rose. The Elvis\'92 eyes rolled up and then snapped back, focused, alight with a feverish certainty Lonnie had seen before only in tourists and in men about to start a fight they couldn\'92t finish.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He sat up with an abruptness that made everyone jump.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He looked around, smiled, and for a horrible second Lonnie recognised the exact shape of the smile: the stage smile. The performing smile. The smile an Elvis wore when he knew he had an audience.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My brothers and sisters,\'94 he said, voice strengthening, \'93we don\'92t have to live like this.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie stared.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Elvis held his hands out\'97palms up, pleading. He looked like a man asking for tips, except what he wanted wasn\'92t money.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93What did you hear, sugar?\'94 Lonnie asked him, keeping her voice low and sharp. \'93What did you hear on that thing?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He turned his head slowly toward her, as if hearing her properly for the first time. \'93Truth,\'94 he said, and his eyes were wet. \'93Harmony. A way home.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie\'92s skin crawled.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bar\'92s alley door banged. A couple of punters had wandered in early, drawn by the open sign and the smell of deep-fried crackers. They took one look at the scene\'97staff clustered like a crime\'97and hesitated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The evangelist Elvis saw them and brightened like a man given purpose.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He stood, unsteady, and began moving toward them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie grabbed his wrist.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He didn\'92t even look at her; he just kept smiling at the punters as if they were lost children.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Lonnie,\'94 one of her staff said quietly. \'93What do we do?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie let go of the evangelist\'92s wrist because she had a rule, and the rule was: you never start a bar fight you can\'92t control, and you never start a religion you can\'92t kill.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She stood up and pointed at the door.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You,\'94 she barked at the junior Elvis. \'93Lock it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The junior Elvis moved, grateful for an instruction that involved something solid. He slid the bolt across with a loud, definitive clack.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The punters on the other side of the door frowned and knocked.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie turned back to the jukebox, to the open cabinet, to the unfamiliar records in their neat little slots, and felt something cold settle behind her ribs.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>This wasn\'92t a tourist prank. This wasn\'92t a bad joke. This was a job.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Somewhere out there, someone had decided the Ghetto needed saving.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie spat on the floor, right where the evangelist had been lying.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Tax,\'94 she said, and then louder, to her staff: \'93No one touches the jukebox. No one sings anything. No one hums. And if any of you so much as whistle, I will personally put you through the glass.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Behind her, the evangelist Elvis began to sing anyway\'97softly at first, a tune he didn\'92t know yesterday and now couldn\'92t stop knowing. The sound threaded through the corridor and into the bar like smoke.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Lonnie closed her eyes for half a second, then opened them and went for her phone.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She had people to call.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Not the Airborne. Not the cops. Not PAPER.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>People who understood what it meant when something changed in the Ghetto and did it wearing a smile.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And, somewhere in the building, the stage lights kept breathing\'97patient as a predator.
